diagnostic_evidence.quote:
Since $\Phi_{d-1}(B_{d})=0$, the generalized B\'ezout's theorem yields the existence of a lambda-matrix $\Phi_{d-2}\in\poly_{d-2}(\R^{b\times b})$, such that  
    \begin{equation*}
        \Phi_{d-1}(\lambda) = (\lambda I-B_{d})\Phi_{d-2}(\lambda) = (\lambda I-\widehat{B}_{d})\Phi_{d-2}(\lambda) \quad \forall\,\lambda\in\R.
    \end{equation*}
